ofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzU5Mjk2NDc2OTI4MDAwMDA=', 'text': 'New Jersey parents, brace yourselves for education standards further plummeting! In June 2024, our Assembly approved a bill that removes the requirement for prospective teachers to pass a basic skills test for certification or program admission, except in specific limited certification cases. Bill was introduced by Democrats but passed with majority on both sides  supporting. Only one republican vote Nay "Rep. Webber, Jay [R]"\n\nBill Title: Removes obstacles to teacher certification for certain teachers; repeals law establishing alternative certificate of eligibility.\n\nSummary of Bill C.18A:26-34.4: (By Grok)\n\nThis bill addresses the requirements for obtaining an instructional certificate in New Jersey:\n\nSection 1a: The State Board of Education is prohibited from mandating that candidates for most instructional certificates pass a Commissioner of Education-approved test that assesses basic skills in reading, writing, and mathematics, such as the Praxis Core Academic Skills for Educators test. The exception is for those seeking a limited certificate of eligibility or a limited certificate of eligibility with advanced standing.\n\nSection 1b: Similarly, the bill prevents the State Board of Education from requiring educator preparation programs approved by the Commissioner to mandate these basic skills tests as a condition for admission into their programs.\n\nSection 2: This section mandates that the State Board of Education must adopt rules and regulations to implement this act, following the procedures set out in the Administrative Procedure Act. \n\nAssembly members ballot details\nhttps://t.co/pshy90LDWU', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [{'display_url': 'legiscan.com/NJ/rollcall/A1…', 'expanded_url': 'https://legiscan.com/NJ/rollcall/A1669/id/1461602', 'url': 'https://t.co/ps